{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on comparing the probabilities of the two lotteries and assessing whether their differences are negligible. If so, the preference is based on the maximum (most favorable) outcome. Lottery A has a maximum outcome of 3000 Euros with 90% probability, while Lottery B has a maximum outcome of 6000 Euros with 45% probability. The probabilities differ substantially: 90% vs. 45% for the positive outcomes, and 10% vs. 55% for receiving nothing. These differences (45 percentage points) are not negligible in most interpretations. Therefore, according to the decision reason, since the probability differences are not negligible, the rule would not trigger a choice based solely on the maximum outcome. However, if the individual perceives the probability differences as negligible, then the reason would prefer Lottery B due to its higher maximum outcome (6000 > 3000).",
