{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The lotteries differ in their outcome structures and associated probabilities. Lottery A offers a certain outcome of 500 Euros (100% probability), while Lottery B offers a 50% chance of 1000 Euros and a 50% chance of 0 Euros. The maximum outcome of Lottery B (1000 Euros) is higher than that of Lottery A (500 Euros). The decision reason focuses on comparing the probabilities of the two lotteries and considers differences in probabilities negligible. Since both lotteries involve different probability distributions, the reason evaluates whether the individual disregards these differences and instead favors the lottery with the higher maximum outcome (Lottery B). Thus, the relevant information for the reason is: (1) the maximum outcomes of each lottery, and (2) the assessment of whether probability differences are treated as negligible.",
